WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.976
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4430.

2
00:00:00.976 --> 00:00:01.951
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.951 --> 00:00:02.276
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Okay.

4
00:00:02.276 --> 00:00:04.228
Alright, today is March 27, 2015.

5
00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:10.407
I am Chelsea Arsenault, representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:10.407 --> 00:00:15.610
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

7
00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.187
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:19.187 --> 00:00:21.138
I'm here today with Mr. M.D.

9
00:00:21.138 --> 00:00:21.789
Haki Vincent.

10
00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:25.366
Thank you so much for agreeing to be interviewed, Mr. Vincent.

11
00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:29.593
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen to twenty minutes.

12
00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:29.919
<v HAKI VINCENT>Okay.

13
00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:36.748
<v ARSENEAULT>We will likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview, if you're interested in continuing on.

14
00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:37.073
<v VINCENT>Yes.

15
00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And could you state your full name, please?

16
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:40.900
<v VINCENT>M.D.

17
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.700
Haki Vincent.

18
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:48.100
<v ARSENEAULT>When and where were you born?

19
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:58.000
<v VINCENT>I was born October 9, 1935, [very loud crackling] Morocco, Africa.

20
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.700
<v ARSENEAULT>Where in Morocco?

21
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.600
<v VINCENT>Casablanca.

22
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.100
<v ARSENEAULT>What are your parents' names?

23
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:16.000
<v VINCENT>Simon Vincent and Juanita Vincent. [creak of a door opening]

24
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.263
<v ARSENEAULT>And grandparents' names?

25
00:01:18.263 --> 00:01:22.035
<v VINCENT>Eli Sr. and Eli Jr.

26
00:01:22.035 --> 00:01:25.807
<v ARSENEAULT>And where are they from?

27
00:01:25.807 --> 00:01:31.088
<v VINCENT>They were from right here in Louisiana.

28
00:01:31.088 --> 00:01:33.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Where in Louisiana?

29
00:01:33.351 --> 00:01:34.105
<v VINCENT>Mossville.

30
00:01:34.105 --> 00:01:37.123
Toxic Town, Mossville, Louisiana.

31
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:43.158
At that time, though, it was, like pristine.

32
00:01:43.158 --> 00:01:43.912
Yes.

33
00:01:43.912 --> 00:01:49.947
<v ARSENEAULT>What's your earliest . . . your earliest memory of Mossville?

34
00:01:49.947 --> 00:01:55.982
<v VINCENT>My earliest memory . . . I can't recall too well,

35
00:01:55.982 --> 00:02:07.298
because I was about . . . maybe six months old when my mother brought me down here.

36
00:02:07.298 --> 00:02:19.368
But I was constantly enlightened or empowered on the history from my father and my uncles.

37
00:02:19.368 --> 00:02:24.649
Because we all . . . I was a gofer.

38
00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:38.982
I was the one who straightened out nails and went for the water and went for anything they needed.

39
00:02:38.982 --> 00:02:42.000
That's a gofer.

40
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.871
<v ARSENEAULT>So you go for?

41
00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:58.510
<v VINCENT>I went for. [Laughs] So that's how I learned the trade of carpentry and housebuilding and many other trades.

42
00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:12.149
So I was well-empowered on the history of Mossville from the 1700s on up until I really experienced it.

43
00:03:12.149 --> 00:03:20.762
When I experienced it, I felt it right here . . . in my heart.

44
00:03:20.762 --> 00:03:21.480
Yes.

45
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.787
<v ARSENEAULT>So your family talked about Mossville . . .

46
00:03:25.787 --> 00:03:27.223
<v VINCENT>Constantly, yeah.

47
00:03:27.223 --> 00:03:28.658
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . to you?

48
00:03:28.658 --> 00:03:32.965
While you were in Africa or?

49
00:03:32.965 --> 00:03:37.990
<v VINCENT>Well, mainly while I was in DC.

50
00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:43.733
That's where I was raised, in Washington DC.

51
00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:54.500
But that's where I got all my information, while we were on jobs and whatnot.

52
00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:03.832
So I was learning the trade and the history at the same time.

53
00:04:03.832 --> 00:04:18.188
From the beginning up until now, it's been a labor of love . . . knowing that your ancestors blessed you with land.

54
00:04:18.188 --> 00:04:18.906
Yes.

55
00:04:18.906 --> 00:04:25.366
So, when I got of age, military took me.

56
00:04:25.366 --> 00:04:39.005
But I couldn't wait to get here, so I had to do four years, three months in the military.

57
00:04:39.005 --> 00:04:46.183
When I got out, then I experienced living here, yes.

58
00:04:46.183 --> 00:05:00.540
But at the same time, I had a long history of government antagonism . . . from the military up until right now.

59
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you when you were drafted?

60
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.508
<v VINCENT>Seventeen.

61
00:05:07.508 --> 00:05:12.592
They drafted me . . . well, they were drafting, in the army.

62
00:05:12.592 --> 00:05:20.725
I decided I didn't want to go in the army because it was too much violence.

63
00:05:20.725 --> 00:05:25.300
So I decided to go in the air force.

64
00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.350
Coming from Chocolate City, Washington DC,

65
00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:39.025
I was amazed to see all these Europeans on the train when I got on the train going to the military.

66
00:05:39.025 --> 00:05:42.075
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you mean, Chocolate City?

67
00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:49.700
<v VINCENT>That's what DC was called at one time, because it was mostly black people there.

68
00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.242
It was called Chocolate City.

69
00:05:52.242 --> 00:05:54.275
You didn't know that?

70
00:05:54.275 --> 00:05:54.783
Yeah.

71
00:05:54.783 --> 00:06:00.883
So . . . I got on the train on the way to basic training.

72
00:06:00.883 --> 00:06:08.000
Oh, by the way, before that . . . When I was sixteen, I had a car.

73
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.100
It was basically given to me, but it was an old Chrysler.

74
00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:21.217
After I got off of work, I cleaned up and went for a ride.

75
00:06:21.217 --> 00:06:24.267
You know, looking for the females.

76
00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:27.317
Two white cops pulled me over.

77
00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:33.925
It wasn't no more than about three or four blocks from my house.

78
00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:37.483
They asked me for my driver's license.

79
00:06:37.483 --> 00:06:38.500
I gave

80
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.533
<c.p>them my driver's license.

81
00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:42.567
They say, "Follow us."

82
00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:47.650
Went around a couple of corners to a dark street.

83
00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:55.275
They brought my driver's license back to me and said, "How much money you got?"

84
00:06:55.275 --> 00:06:56.800
Yes, they did.

85
00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:07.983
I don't remember how much I had then, but I took it out and said . . . You know, they took what they wanted.

86
00:07:07.983 --> 00:07:10.017
Left me the rest.

87
00:07:10.017 --> 00:07:15.100
Told me, "We don't want to see you no more."

88
00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.133
I was so outdone.

89
00:07:17.133 --> 00:07:22.725
I didn't tell nobody that for years, because . . . You know why?

90
00:07:22.725 --> 00:07:26.283
I felt that they wouldn't believe me.

91
00:07:26.283 --> 00:07:30.858
Matter of fact, I knew they wouldn't believe me.

92
00:07:30.858 --> 00:07:38.483
From that point on, I been had a bad taste in my mouth for criminals.

93
00:07:38.483 --> 00:07:42.550
Especially in costumes. [Laughs] So, like I said,

94
00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:56.783
when I got on the train and saw all of these white faces on the train . . . So you kind of know what was back here in my head.

95
00:07:56.783 --> 00:07:59.833
I'm in trouble. [Laughs] Big trouble.

96
00:07:59.833 --> 00:08:07.458
I was mad anyway, because they were forcing me to go into somebody else's war.

97
00:08:07.458 --> 00:08:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What war was this?

98
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:19.925
<v VINCENT>World War . . . I was getting ready to say World War II, but I was ten. [laughs] It was Korean War.

99
00:08:19.925 --> 00:08:23.399
<v ARSENEAULT>I hope it wasn't World War II.

100
00:08:23.399 --> 00:08:25.881
<v VINCENT>It was World War II.

101
00:08:25.881 --> 00:08:29.354
I was in World War II, too.

102
00:08:29.354 --> 00:08:35.806
I was working for the government, collecting papers, helping them with the war.

103
00:08:35.806 --> 00:08:43.746
I remember that just as clear, because our school collected enough papers to buy a Jeep.

104
00:08:43.746 --> 00:08:52.183
When they brought the Jeep to the school and showed it to the people, I said, "Whoa!

105
00:08:52.183 --> 00:08:54.664
I got me a Jeep."

106
00:08:54.664 --> 00:09:02.108
Hard as I worked, collecting papers and whatnot . . . I had some interest in that Jeep.

107
00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:05.582
That's the way I felt, you know.

108
00:09:05.582 --> 00:09:06.575
That wasn't

109
00:09:06.575 --> 00:09:13.522
<c.p>happening. [Laughs] They wouldn't even let me ride in it. [Laughs] But anyway . . . So,

110
00:09:13.522 --> 00:09:19.974
when I got into the military, I did nothing to help the military.

111
00:09:19.974 --> 00:09:21.959
Need I say more?

112
00:09:21.959 --> 00:09:23.944
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your . . . MOS?

113
00:09:23.944 --> 00:09:26.425
<v VINCENT>I was inspecting survival equipment.

114
00:09:26.425 --> 00:09:31.884
Yes . . . and every other little thing they demanded you to do.

115
00:09:31.884 --> 00:09:40.817
Any other assigned duties . . . That's in most contracts with the government, if y'all read the fine little lines.

116
00:09:40.817 --> 00:09:46.276
Any other assign . . . So, they got trash work they need done,

117
00:09:46.276 --> 00:09:55.705
look out you might be the one. [Laughs] My four years, three months in the military was an experience.

118
00:09:55.705 --> 00:10:03.149
When I got out of the military . . . When I was in there, I got married.

119
00:10:03.149 --> 00:10:04.638
Had three children.

120
00:10:04.638 --> 00:10:09.104
I got out the marriage . . . lasted about seven years.

121
00:10:09.104 --> 00:10:14.067
When I got out, it in was in the sixties.

122
00:10:14.067 --> 00:10:23.000
I went to military . . . I went to school, to collect some of my money from the military.

123
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.027
Then, here comes nineteen sixty eight.

124
00:10:26.027 --> 00:10:28.549
Martin Luther King got killed.

125
00:10:28.549 --> 00:10:31.575
That's when all the actions started.

126
00:10:31.575 --> 00:10:36.619
Two cops . . . Two white cops pulled me over one night.

127
00:10:36.619 --> 00:10:41.159
October . . . I mean, not October . . . June, June 6, '68.

128
00:10:41.159 --> 00:10:43.681
About twelve o'clock at night.

129
00:10:43.681 --> 00:10:51.248
Pulled me over, asking about my motorcycle . . . I was about a block from my house.

130
00:10:51.248 --> 00:10:54.274
They asked me for my ID.

131
00:10:54.274 --> 00:10:58.310
I said, "My ID is in my house.

132
00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.327
I live right there."

133
00:11:00.327 --> 00:11:05.372
So, they wouldn't allow me to go get my ID.

134
00:11:05.372 --> 00:11:08.398
They said, "Get in the car.

135
00:11:08.398 --> 00:11:10.416
You're going with us."

136
00:11:10.416 --> 00:11:17.982
I said, "I'm not getting in the car with you and leave my motorcycle here."

137
00:11:17.982 --> 00:11:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was this?

138
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.754
<v VINCENT>This was a block from my house . . . Hayes Street, Washington DC.

139
00:11:24.754 --> 00:11:27.347
I was talking to a neighbor.

140
00:11:27.347 --> 00:11:36.422
We debated back and forth for a minute there, and they said, "Okay, you can ride the bike to the precinct."

141
00:11:36.422 --> 00:11:42.472
So I walked around the corner and saw that she got in the house.

142
00:11:42.472 --> 00:11:47.226
I got on the bike and started heading for the precinct.

143
00:11:47.226 --> 00:11:48.955
Stopped at a stoplight.

144
00:11:48.955 --> 00:11:55.005
They pulled up beside me and asked me do I know where it was?

145
00:11:55.005 --> 00:11:56.302
I said, "Yeah."

146
00:11:56.302 --> 00:12:04.513
He laid his gun on the seat beside him, said, "We going to be following you, don't try nothing."

147
00:12:04.513 --> 00:12:09.266
So I start thinking, I'm going to the precinct with them.

148
00:12:09.266 --> 00:12:11.427
I don't have no ID.

149
00:12:11.427 --> 00:12:14.452
They still don't know who I am.

150
00:12:14.452 --> 00:12:17.045
So I decided not to go.

151
00:12:17.045 --> 00:12:20.503
I turned on off and headed back home.

152
00:12:20.503 --> 00:12:23.960
The first block I went, they start shooting.

153
00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.256
The first block.

154
00:12:25.256 --> 00:12:29.578
So, there was maybe about ten blocks from my house.

155
00:12:29.578 --> 00:12:32.171
They were shooting all the way.

156
00:12:32.171 --> 00:12:40.814
Matter of fact, the shotgun rider got the driver's gun and started using it because he ran out of bullets.

157
00:12:40.814 --> 00:12:46.432
But he shot me in the back, about a block from my house.

158
00:12:46.432 --> 00:12:55.940
So when I made a wide U-turn to go to my house, I hit some railroad tracks and it threw me off.

159
00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:59.829
I got up and was limping towards the house.

160
00:12:59.829 --> 00:13:02.422
The passenger was right behind me.

161
00:13:02.422 --> 00:13:11.065
He came up, and I went down in this little area way . . . about maybe four feet wide, four steps down.

162
00:13:11.065 --> 00:13:16.683
I was knocking on the door, for my brother to open the door.

163
00:13:16.683 --> 00:13:22.302
The cop that was behind me got in between me and the door.

164
00:13:22.302 --> 00:13:28.352
He pushed me back against the wall, and I was kicking on the door.

165
00:13:28.352 --> 00:13:30.945
He had his gun on me.

166
00:13:30.945 --> 00:13:36.563
When the driver came to the little area way . . . He had a flashlight.

167
00:13:36.563 --> 00:13:40.884
He shot me in the right ankle with a Derringer.

168
00:13:40.884 --> 00:13:49.528
I fell down on my left side, and my head was on about the second step . . . second or third step.

169
00:13:49.528 --> 00:13:52.553
He started kicking me in the head.

170
00:13:52.553 --> 00:13:59.467
The one that was in front of me shot me point-blank in the chest right here.

171
00:13:59.467 --> 00:14:06.814
When he shot me in the chest, I started cussing him out and spitting blood on him.

172
00:14:06.814 --> 00:14:13.296
At that time, my brother opened the door . . . He turned kind of on my brother.

173
00:14:13.296 --> 00:14:18.915
My other brother came to the top of the steps, and he was

174
00:14:18.915 --> 00:14:23.236
<c.p>coming down . . . The cop said, "You can't come down here."

175
00:14:23.236 --> 00:14:25.829
He said, "You're a damn liar.

176
00:14:25.829 --> 00:14:28.422
That's my brother you got there."

177
00:14:28.422 --> 00:14:31.015
So all hell broke loose then.

178
00:14:31.015 --> 00:14:34.905
The rest of the cops came, the ambulance came.

179
00:14:34.905 --> 00:14:41.387
They handcuffed me . . . The handcuffs were so tight, I couldn't even feel the bullet wounds.

180
00:14:41.387 --> 00:14:43.980
That's how tight the handcuffs were.

181
00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:51.759
I was in the ambulance going to hospital, and I told the cop, "These cuffs are too tight."

182
00:14:51.759 --> 00:14:56.080
The cop just looked at me like a black snake.

183
00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:01.266
So, I told the attendant in the ambulance, "Feel these cuffs, man.

184
00:15:01.266 --> 00:15:02.563
They're too tight."

185
00:15:02.563 --> 00:15:08.613
He checked them, and he told the cop, "You need to loosen them up."

186
00:15:08.613 --> 00:15:11.638
That's when the cop loosened them up.

187
00:15:11.638 --> 00:15:13.799
We got to the hospital.

188
00:15:13.799 --> 00:15:18.985
Here come cops, detective, and everything . . . Running in there, asking me questions.

189
00:15:18.985 --> 00:15:23.739
One of them lit a cigarette and stuck in my mouth.

190
00:15:23.739 --> 00:15:31.085
I spit it at him . . . The doctor told him they had to get out there, you know.

191
00:15:31.085 --> 00:15:33.678
He was asking me, "What happened?"

192
00:15:33.678 --> 00:15:38.000
I said, "You ask your racist coworkers what happened."

193
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:46.558
After that . . . They had a cop at my door the whole time I was in the hospital.

194
00:15:46.558 --> 00:15:50.585
I got out . . . They took me to court.

195
00:15:50.585 --> 00:15:55.619
The cops didn't show up, so they dropped the charges.

196
00:15:55.619 --> 00:16:02.163
They had a charge against me for assault on a cop or something.

197
00:16:02.163 --> 00:16:07.197
The cops didn't show up, so the judge dismissed it.

198
00:16:07.197 --> 00:16:13.741
When he dismissed the charge, they reinstated the charge or whatever they do.

199
00:16:13.741 --> 00:16:23.810
And they were back at my house that night, with the guns drawn and all around the house, you know.

200
00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:28.340
On the . . . The next year, which would be sixty-nine,

201
00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:36.898
we filed a suit against the cops and the government for two and a half million dollars.

202
00:16:36.898 --> 00:16:46.966
When they found out that suit was against . . . When they found out that I didn't even have a juvenile record,

203
00:16:46.966 --> 00:16:51.497
no criminal record whatsoever . . . they came back, October tenth.

204
00:16:51.497 --> 00:16:54.014
What day was I born?

205
00:16:54.014 --> 00:16:55.524
October ninth, right?

206
00:16:55.524 --> 00:16:59.551
They came back October tenth with the no-knock.

207
00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:08.109
At that time, they were killing [Black] Panther Party members all over the country . . . in bed, sleep.

208
00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:09.619
Justified killing them.

209
00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:13.646
So they tried that on me . . . didn't work.

210
00:17:13.646 --> 00:17:21.701
When I heard them breaking my door in . . . And I sleep naked . . . no clothes . . . a gun

211
00:17:21.701 --> 00:17:27.238
<c.p>under my pillow, a gun in every corner of my house.

212
00:17:27.238 --> 00:17:36.803
When I heard them breaking my door in, I jumped straight up, grabbed my .38 from under my pillow,

213
00:17:36.803 --> 00:17:46.871
heading for the front door . . . Put my .38 in my left hand. [phone ringing] When I got to my bathroom,

214
00:17:46.871 --> 00:17:52.912
I reached in on the shelf and grabbed my .45 . . . cocked it.

215
00:17:52.912 --> 00:18:01.469
When I got to the corner of the door, they were this far . . . this close to me.

216
00:18:01.469 --> 00:18:02.476
Eight feet.

217
00:18:02.476 --> 00:18:06.000
Door burst open about that wide.

218
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:16.068
The first shot they fired out of this twelve-gauge double-aught buck shotgun was that close to my head . . . that was the first shot.

219
00:18:16.068 --> 00:18:20.445
After they fired that shot, I went into a squat.

220
00:18:20.445 --> 00:18:23.509
The other four shots was right here.

221
00:18:23.509 --> 00:18:32.263
They had the drugs with them, because after they ran out, I shot . . . When they were shooting the four shots,

222
00:18:32.263 --> 00:18:39.267
I shot two rounds out of that .45. [slaps hand] They were gone with the sound.

223
00:18:39.267 --> 00:18:40.142
Trust me.

224
00:18:40.142 --> 00:18:50.648
When the sound left, they were with it. [laughs] They went outside, and they shot thirteen canisters of tear gas through the front window.

225
00:18:50.648 --> 00:18:55.463
I was in there with a wet towel on my face.

226
00:18:55.463 --> 00:19:00.278
We stayed in there . . . I stayed in there for three hours.

227
00:19:00.278 --> 00:19:01.591
We were negotiating.

228
00:19:01.591 --> 00:19:03.342
"Where's your search warrant?

229
00:19:03.342 --> 00:19:05.093
What you here for?"

230
00:19:05.093 --> 00:19:12.972
Nobody knew where the search warrant was because they dropped it when they were running down the steps.

231
00:19:12.972 --> 00:19:17.786
So they say, "Can anybody come in and talk to you?"

232
00:19:17.786 --> 00:19:23.039
I say, "My brother can come in, but anybody else is dead."

233
00:19:23.039 --> 00:19:33.107
My brother came in . . . When he saw the attaché case laying there, he opened it up and looked in it and he frowned.

234
00:19:33.107 --> 00:19:37.046
Then he leaned it over so I could see.

235
00:19:37.046 --> 00:19:40.110
They had drugs and papers in it.

236
00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:42.737
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they trying to frame you?

237
00:19:42.737 --> 00:19:43.174
<v VINCENT>Absolutely.

238
00:19:43.174 --> 00:19:49.302
They couldn't kill me, so they had to do the next thing . . . Frame me.

239
00:19:49.302 --> 00:19:54.555
They wanted to kill me and frame me at the same time.

240
00:19:54.555 --> 00:19:57.181
So, we talked about it and

241
00:19:57.181 --> 00:20:01.559
<c.p>whatnot . . . and the whole community was around by that time.

242
00:20:01.559 --> 00:20:09.000
Because they knew, you know, the history . . . I had got shot by the cops, what now.

243
00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:16.602
<v ARSENEAULT>We would love to sit down with you again and get a longer interview.

244
00:20:16.602 --> 00:20:17.145
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

245
00:20:17.145 --> 00:20:23.661
<v ARSENEAULT>We have to wrap it up, just because we have people waiting.

246
00:20:23.661 --> 00:20:24.204
<v VINCENT>Okay.

247
00:20:24.204 --> 00:20:35.065
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask you one last question, relating to Mossville . . . and kind of what this community means to you.

248
00:20:35.065 --> 00:20:38.323
<v VINCENT>It means that . . . We have been,

249
00:20:38.323 --> 00:20:47.554
us as a people here . . . I'm talking about African people . . . have been duped so bad as criminal.

250
00:20:47.554 --> 00:20:53.527
Because this system of capitalism has become a system of imperialism.

251
00:20:53.527 --> 00:21:04.930
I know that we should be doing better than we are, as a people here in this so-called filthy rich country.

252
00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:14.704
It means to me that . . . We are better than the ones who doing what they're doing to us.

253
00:21:14.704 --> 00:21:17.419
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, our time is up.

254
00:21:17.419 --> 00:21:17.962
<v VINCENT>Alright.

255
00:21:17.962 --> 00:21:29.366
<v ARSENEAULT>On behalf of the center and the museum, I just want to thank you so much for sharing that with us.

256
00:21:29.366 --> 00:21:35.339
Did you want to add anything before we close it up?

257
00:21:35.339 --> 00:21:42.398
<v VINCENT>Only thing I want to add . . . That's just the tip of the iceberg.

258
00:21:42.398 --> 00:21:43.484
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, definitely.

259
00:21:43.484 --> 00:21:44.027
<v VINCENT>[Laughs]

260
00:21:44.027 --> 00:21:46.742
<v ARSENEAULT>And that concludes our interview.

261
00:21:46.742 --> 00:21:48.914
Thank you so much.

262
00:21:48.914 --> 00:21:50.000
<v VINCENT>Alright.

263
00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:50.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4430.

264
00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:50.200
End Session I.]
